How to Upgrade a Kubernetes (K8s) Cluster 🚀

Upgrading a Kubernetes cluster may sound scary, but it’s actually step-by-step and safe if done correctly. Here’s a simple explanation + LinkedIn-ready post 👇

🧠 What does “K8s Upgrade” mean?
It means updating:

Kubernetes control plane (API Server, Scheduler, Controller)

Kubernetes worker nodes
to a newer supported version without breaking running applications.

Easy Steps to Upgrade a Kubernetes Cluster
Step 1: Check Current Version
kubectl version
Know where you are before moving forward.

Step 2: Read Release Notes
Check what’s deprecated or removed

Never skip minor versions (e.g., 1.27 → 1.28 → 1.29)

Step 3: Take Backup (Very Important)
Backup etcd (cluster data)

Backup manifests & configs
 This is your safety net.

Step 4: Upgrade Control Plane First
Upgrade API Server, Controller, Scheduler

Cluster keeps running during this step
 Applications are still accessible

Step 5: Upgrade Worker Nodes (One by One)
Drain node (move pods safely)

kubectl drain <node-name> --ignore-daemonsets
Upgrade node Kubernetes version

Uncordon node

kubectl uncordon <node-name>
 Repeat for each node (zero downtime if done properly)

Step 6: Verify Cluster Health
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A
Make sure everything is Running.

Step 7: Monitor Applications
Check logs, metrics, alerts

Confirm no performance issues

 Managed K8s (EKS / AKS / GKE) – Even Easier
Control plane upgrade is 1 click / 1 command

Nodes can be upgraded via node groups

Cloud provider handles complexity

💡 Golden Rule:
✔ Control Plane first
✔ Worker Nodes later
✔ One node at a time = zero downtime

☁️ On EKS / AKS / GKE, upgrades are even simpler with managed workflows.
